  "question": {
    "text": "Boron cannot form which one of the following anions?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "MT17m",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{BH}_4^{-}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "MsUQu",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{OH})_4^{-}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FWlsz",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{BO}_2^{-}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "6S84Z",
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{BF}_6^{3-}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "As Boron has only four orbitals in the valence shell ( i.e. $2 s, 2 p_x, 2 p_y \\& 2 p_z$ ) it can show a maximum valency of four only.\nTherefore $\\left[\\mathrm{BF}_6\\right]^{3-}$ is not possible",
    "image": null
  },
